---
// Figure: the round trips of opening a connection, against reusing a pooled one.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
import './clients.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="clients-connect" title="What a new connection costs before the first command" controls="steps"
  caption="A sequence of steps, not a timed simulation. The TCP and TLS 1.3 messages follow those protocols' handshakes and are shown, not captured; HELLO with AUTH is one Redis command (Redis 6.0 and later). The text gives the times I measured.">
  <Choice slot="params" name="mode" label="For each request, the application" value="fresh"
    options={[{ value: 'fresh', label: 'opens a new connection' }, { value: 'pooled', label: 'reuses a pooled one' }]} />
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>Opening a new connection: the TCP handshake takes one round trip, the TLS 1.3 handshake another, HELLO with credentials a third, and only then does the GET go out, so the value arrives after four round trips.</p>
    <p>Reusing a pooled connection: the application borrows a connection that is already open, secured and authenticated, sends the GET and has its value after one round trip.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { switchSequence } from './switch-sequence.mjs';
  import { connectSpecs } from './sequences.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="clients-connect"]').forEach(root => switchSequence(root, connectSpecs, 'mode'));
</script>
